\section{Plan Relocation Before Reusing Addresses}

Sliding mark-compact and semispace copying both move objects.
The difference is that a sliding collector packs live objects
within their original region, potentially overlapping old
and new extents. It first determines liveness, then assigns
destinations, and then rewrites references and moves bytes.
The relocation table is keyed by old object starts throughout
reference repair. Aho and colleagues describe this use of
increasing source order to slide objects toward the lower end
of the heap \cite{aho2007compilers}. The scheme saves a separate
destination region, not all auxiliary storage: marking still
needs work memory and relocation needs a representation.

Consider one aligned region with fixed-size headers and several
variable-size live objects. The placements below are offsets in
the same region; \(C\) moves into a range that includes some
of its own old bytes. The old address of \(C\) will eventually
be inside the relocated \(C\) rather than at its start. An address
inside a parseable heap is not necessarily a valid object reference.

\begin{center}
\begin{tabular}{lrrr}
\hline
Chunk & Old start & Bytes & New start \\
\hline
\(A\), live & 0 & 32 & 0 \\
Gap & 32 & 16 & --- \\
\(B\), live & 48 & 32 & 32 \\
\(C\), live & 80 & 48 & 64 \\
Gap & 128 & 32 & --- \\
\hline
\end{tabular}
\end{center}

\Cref{fig:visual-414} depicts the slide. With uniform alignment,
padding is already included in each extent, so the prefix sum
of live extents produces another aligned start each time.
For more varied alignment requirements, include any resulting
gaps in the physical walk format rather than assuming the
packed region has no intervening space.

