% Annotated walkthrough
\section{Use an Immutable Old-Address Plan}

\begin{verbatim}
compact(region, roots):
    mark_strong_closure(roots)
    plan := empty_sequence()
    dst := region.begin
    for chunk in walk_old_chunks(region):
        if chunk.allocated and marked(chunk.start):
            plan.append(chunk.start, dst, chunk.extent)
            dst += chunk.extent

    mapping := index_old_starts(plan)
    for root in roots: repair_slot_once(root, mapping)
    for old, new, bytes in plan:
        for slot in strong_slots(object_at(old)):
            repair_slot_once(slot, mapping)

    for old, new, bytes in plan:
        move_overlapping_bytes(new, old, bytes)
    rebuild_metadata(region, plan, dst)
\end{verbatim}

The first two loops that use the plan rewrite the root and
payload slots while they still occupy old locations. For a
nonnull slot into the compacted region, the old address must
be in the mapping, because a strong edge cannot lead from a
survivor to a dead object. Treat a missing key as a collector
bug. A value pointing out of the region is unchanged, though
outside objects that point into the region still need to
contribute incoming slots. Weak and derived references need
separate handling. The algorithm edits each physical slot
once. If a slot has been repaired, its new address might also
be a valid key in the mapping; a second application could
move the reference a second time.

In source order, the end of each destination extent is no
greater than the end of its source extent. The source of any
later object starts at or past that boundary, so the current
move cannot overwrite an unread later object. It can overlap
itself; use an overlap-safe byte move rather than a copy
primitive that assumes disjoint ranges. The move uses the
saved extent, not a fresh heap walker trying to recognize
headers in a heap already half compacted. Allowing either a
mutator or unrelated heap verifier to walk this intermediate
state would break the interpretation of addresses.

\section{Support Pins Without Moving Through Them}

An object can be live and also forbidden to move, for example
while an external API holds its raw address. One policy treats
pinned objects as barriers: perform a slide independently in
each interval between them and keep their addresses as
identity entries in the relocation map. Reference repair still
scans the fields of pinned objects; pinning their addresses
does not pin their referents. This sacrifices the guarantee
of one large free suffix. If a pinned word or derived address
is the only evidence of liveness, register a proper root as
well. A nonmoving pool is another design that exchanges more
frequent edge bookkeeping for simpler layouts in movable
regions.

A side table can store a destination delta, an absolute
address, or prefix information from which the destination is
computed. Measure its lookup traffic during reference repair.
Using the first word of every live object as temporary storage
saves separate entries only if descriptor and extent information
remain recoverable. The plan above values clarity over memory
economy. Benchmark it with a dense live heap: calculating
destinations and rewriting the graph may dominate even if
most objects barely move and some remain at the same address.

\section{Test the Difference Between an Address and Its Owner}

Start with an object whose identity hash has already been
published, put it in a hash table, and run a slide that moves
it. The post-slide lookup must still work. If hashing is based
on the address, either preserve an assigned hash in the object
or design a table protocol that allows rehashing when the
language permits it. Tests for raw reference equality alone do
not catch a change in a previously visible hash.

Construct a relocation mapping in which one new start equals
another object's old start, deliberately list one root slot
twice, and assert that the root visitor diagnoses or normalizes
the duplicate. Then test a large object sliding only one
alignment unit, a dead region, and pinning in the middle of a
hole-filled region. Verify metadata after moving: starts, size
records, mark state, and any address-indexed intergenerational
tracking must describe the resulting heap, not the old
configuration with patched payloads.

\section{Reserve Side Metadata for Much or Little Motion}

A plan entry per survivor is straightforward but can be
expensive when the region contains a large number of tiny
objects. For a region with several million 16-byte objects,
three eight-byte fields per entry would require more space
than the live objects themselves. A more compact design
stores a destination prefix at fixed spatial boundaries and
uses a bitmap or a short walk to sum aligned live extents
inside the relevant interval. The tradeoff is more work per
lookup, which is multiplied by the number of references,
not just the number of moved objects. Measure alias-heavy
graphs alongside graphs of the same size with fewer edges.

Side metadata does not have to cover every old byte at the
same granularity. If pages already carry object-start maps,
the old offset can first identify a page and then a compact
array indexed by the rank of that start on the page. An
object may cross a page boundary; count its extent at its
start, and do not add it again when processing the next page.
Conversely, if the relocation policy forbids crossing a page
boundary at the destination, that policy adds padding into
the prefix calculation. Either decision needs to agree with
the overlap proof and the new heap walker.

Failure to reserve the plan must be dealt with before modifying
root or object slots. In a collector with a mark-sweep
fallback, it is possible to reuse the mark state and sweep
without moving, provided the object layout is still intact
and the root pass has not already overwritten addresses.
Test this by injecting a reservation failure during planning.
The failure cannot be handled by falling back to sweeping once
references have been rewritten to destinations that do not
yet contain the corresponding live objects.
Run the same low-memory fixture on a heap with no garbage.
Success at preserving that heap does not turn an impossible
allocation into a possible one; the allocator still needs a
distinct exhaustion path.
